        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Theocracy</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 515 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Power held by</span><span class="info-stat-val">A religious authority, whether a priesthood, a supreme cleric or a ruler held to be divinely guided, with the final say over law and government</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Legitimacy from</span><span class="info-stat-val">The claim that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> belongs to God or the gods, whose will is known through scripture, revelation, sacred law or an inspired leader</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How rulers are chosen</span><span class="info-stat-val">Usually by religious bodies rather than voters: popes are elected by cardinals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a> are found as reincarnations, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>’s Supreme Leader is chosen by an assembly of jurists</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Famous examples</span><span class="info-stat-val">Judea under the high priests, the Papal States, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> under the Ganden Phodrang, the Prince-Bishopric of Montenegro, the Islamic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, Vatican City</span></div>
