    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The word theocracy, “rule of God”, was coined by the Jewish historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> in the 1st century CE for a people whose law came from God and was administered by priests.</li><li>Popes ruled the Papal States from 756 to 1870, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> from 1642 to 1959, and since 1979 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>’s constitution has placed a senior Islamic jurist at the head of the state.</li><li>Scholars debate which states count as theocracies: many distinguish rule by clergy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-kingship">divine kingship</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> bound by religious law, and from states that only have an official religion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1750 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma">Marthanda Varma</a>, king of Travancore in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, gave his kingdom to the god Padmanabha, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>; he and his successors then ruled as the deity’s servants, with the title Padmanabhadasa.</p></aside>
    </section>

