    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 515 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> is dedicated; a priest-led Judean community forms around it under Persian rule</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 95 CE</span><span class="rf-text">In Against Apion the Jewish historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> coins “theocracy” for a constitution that gave all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to God</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">756</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> grants the pope former Byzantine lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, founding the Papal States</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1642</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a> confers authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a>, founding the Ganden Phodrang government</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1870</span><span class="rf-text">Italian troops take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 20 September, ending the Papal States</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1929</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a> creates Vatican City, where the pope holds all legislative, executive and judicial power</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1959</span><span class="rf-text">After the Tibetan uprising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> flees to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> government is dissolved</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1979</span><span class="rf-text">A revolution and a new constitution make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> an Islamic Republic headed by a senior Shia jurist</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kashag"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kashag.jpg" alt="Kashag" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kashag">Kashag</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_135-S-13-11-31,_Tibetexpedition,_Minister.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ernst Schäfer, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Theocracy is government in which a god or gods are recognised as the supreme authority and human intermediaries, whether priests, a sacred ruler or interpreters of religious law, govern in their name. Its defining claim concerns <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>: law comes from revelation rather than from a king’s will or the people’s consent, and those who rule claim to know and apply it. Scholars distinguish several types. In a hierocracy a hierarchy of clergy governs directly, as cardinals and prelates did in the Papal States. In an ecclesiocracy religious leaders run the state without claiming to receive revelation themselves. In other cases the decisive authority is a body of sacred law, interpreted by trained scholars, which binds rulers as well as subjects.</p><p>Institutions varied with the faith. The pope ruled the Papal States as a sovereign, governing the provinces through cardinal legates and the state through congregations of cardinals, and in Vatican City he still holds full legislative, executive and judicial power. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> headed a government, the Ganden Phodrang, in which a council of ministers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashag">Kashag</a>, and parallel ranks of monk and lay officials administered the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>’s constitution of 1979 combines elected institutions, a president and a parliament, with a Supreme Leader who must be an expert in Islamic law. He is chosen by the Assembly of Experts, a body of jurists, and the Guardian Council, half of whose twelve members are clerical jurists, can reject laws passed by parliament and bar candidates from elections.</p><p>Theocracy differs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-kingship">divine kingship</a>, in which the ruler himself is sacred, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> or the emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, though the two often overlap. It differs too from a state with an official religion that is governed by secular rulers under secular law: many monarchies with established churches are not theocracies. Nor is every ruler who claims divine approval a theocrat. Medieval kings anointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> still ruled by dynastic right, and the Byzantine emperors, though held to be God’s representatives, were lay rulers who presided over the affairs of the church; historians still debate whether their empire should be called a theocracy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/josephus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josephus.jpg" alt="Josephus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph,_son_of_Gorian,_called_Flavius_Josephus_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scan by NYPL, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rule in the name of the gods is as old as the state. Mesopotamian kings governed as servants of their cities’ gods, and the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> was identified with the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> and mediated between gods and people. Earlier scholars pictured the Sumerian cities as temple-states run by priest-kings, but specialists have largely abandoned that picture. Some historians call the Shang state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, whose kings divined the will of their ancestors and of the high god Shangdi on oracle bones, a theocracy, but most treat such sacred monarchies as forerunners of theocracy rather than examples of it.</p><p>The first polity to which the word was applied grew up in Judea after the Babylonian exile. When the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> allowed the exiles to return in 538 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> was rebuilt and dedicated around 515 BCE, and the community of the Persian province of Yehud took shape around it, governed by the Torah, the law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, which became its constitution. Beside the governors appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> the high priest grew in authority. By about 300 BCE the Greek writer Hecataeus of Abdera described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> as a people without a king, who entrusted authority to the priest judged foremost in wisdom and virtue. How much civil power the high priests held under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> is debated, but under Ptolemaic and Seleucid rule they spoke for the nation, and the Hasmonean high priests who won its independence in the 140s BCE later took the title of king as well.</p><p>The word itself was coined by the Jewish historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Flavius Josephus</a> in Against Apion, written in the 90s CE. Some peoples, he wrote, had entrusted power to monarchies, others to oligarchies or to the masses, but Moses had given his people a constitution that might be called, “if a forced expression be permitted”, a theocracy, “placing all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and authority in the hands of God”, in H. St. J. Thackeray’s translation. The word entered English in 1622, meaning priestly government under divine inspiration as in ancient Israel. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> it took on a hostile sense, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baruch-spinoza">Baruch Spinoza</a>’s Theological-Political Treatise (1670) used the Hebrew state as a case study in how sovereignty shared with priests breeds strife.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama.jpg" alt="5th Dalai Lama" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thangka_of_the_5th_Dalai_Lama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> religious and political authority were closely bound together. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> held that its emperor, as God’s representative, should rule in harmony with the church, a relationship called symphonia; Steven Runciman wrote that its constitution rested on the conviction that it was “the earthly copy of the Kingdom of Heaven”. After Muhammad’s death in 632 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> led the Muslim community as his successors. Whether the caliphate was a theocracy is debated: Patricia Crone and Martin Hinds argued that the early caliphs claimed religious authority as God’s deputies, an authority that later passed to the scholars, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, while others stress that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> has no ordained clergy and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> theory bound the caliph to a law he did not make. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> the popes became temporal rulers when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> granted them former Byzantine lands in 756, and the Papal States reached their greatest extent in 1649.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> era produced short-lived and disputed experiments. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-savonarola">Girolamo Savonarola</a> dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1494 to 1498; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> ruled Münster as a “New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>” until its prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> retook the city in 1535; and historians still debate whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, where a consistory of ministers and elders supervised morals from 1541, was a theocracy. In Montenegro the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> bishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a> ruled as prince-bishops from 1516 to 1852. In 1642 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a> conferred authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a>, whose Ganden Phodrang government ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> under Mongol and, from 1720, Qing protection. In 1750 the king of Travancore in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> dedicated his kingdom to the god Padmanabha and ruled as his servant.</p><p>The modern era brought both endings and new foundations. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> (1804–1903), and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan">Hong Xiuquan</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> Heavenly Kingdom (1851–1864) fought the Qing in one of the most destructive wars in history. The Papal States lost most of their lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> in 1860 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> itself on 20 September 1870; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> refused to accept the loss until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a> of 1929 created Vatican City. Tibet, independent in practice from 1913, came under the rule of the People’s Republic of China in 1951; after the uprising of 1959 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> fled to India and the Lhasa government was dissolved. In 1979 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>’s revolution created an Islamic Republic on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a>’s doctrine of the guardianship of the Islamic jurist, and the Taliban ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> as an Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> from 1996 to 2001.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii.jpg" alt="Pope Boniface VIII" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_-_Bonifatius_VIII_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giotto, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Defenders of theocracy have argued that it gives a people the best law and the strongest bond. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> claimed that the Jewish constitution excelled all others because its laws came from God, did not change with the whims of rulers, and were taught to every member of the people, so that piety itself was the aim of government. Medieval papal writers held that spiritual authority must guide temporal power because the salvation of souls mattered more than worldly welfare, a claim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> pressed in the bull Unam Sanctam (1302). Later popes argued that the Holy See needed a territory of its own to stay independent of every secular government: Pius IX rejected the Law of Guarantees offered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1871, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a> of 1929 secured that independence in Vatican City.</p><p>Islamic political thought made the case that a ruler bound by sacred law could not become a law unto himself. The 11th-century jurist al-Mawardi held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> existed to uphold the faith and justice, and that a ruler who became unjust could be removed; jurists taught that caliphs too stood under the law, and on occasion caliphs appeared before judges. The legal scholar Noah Feldman has argued that in the classical Islamic constitution the scholars who guarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> checked the ruler, and that the decline of their role in the 19th century removed a restraint on executive power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a> argued in lectures given at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a> in 1970 that only a just jurist could ensure that government followed God’s law rather than the interests of kings or foreign powers.</p><p>Supporters have also pointed to stability and cohesion. A shared faith could unite diverse peoples under one law, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of Hausaland and beyond under a single caliph. The Ganden Phodrang governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> for more than three centuries and sustained its great monasteries. The monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> have run their own community since a charter of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> in 972, and Vatican City has been stable since 1929. Defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>’s system point to its mixture of clerical guardianship with an elected president and parliament, which they present as a religious form of popular government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei.jpg" alt="Galileo Galilei" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galileo_Galilei_(1564-1642)_RMG_BHC2700.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Justus Sustermans, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Critics argue that theocracy puts power beyond accountability: a ruler who speaks for God cannot be voted out, and dissent becomes heresy. Spinoza argued that the Hebrew state decayed once priests gained the right to interpret its laws, and that mixing religious authority with political power bred sectarian conflict; he wanted the sovereign to control public worship and citizens to be free to think as they pleased. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>’s A Letter Concerning Toleration (1689) held that the civil magistrate’s power does not extend to the salvation of souls, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>’s “wall of separation between Church and State” (1802) became the American shorthand for the opposite principle. Within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the Egyptian scholar Ali Abdel Raziq argued in 1925 that Islam prescribes no particular form of government and that rulers had used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> as a religious shield for their thrones.</p><p>History supplied many examples. The Papal States became notorious for misgovernment and debt, and the risings of 1831 and 1848–1849 against clerical rule were put down by Austrian and French troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> kept the last Jewish ghetto in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, and in 1858 papal police in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> took the six-year-old Edgardo Mortara from his Jewish parents because a servant claimed to have baptised him secretly. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Roman Inquisition</a> had Giordano Bruno burned in Rome in 1600 and tried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a> in 1633. Münster’s Anabaptist kingdom punished breaches of the Ten Commandments with death, and Savonarola’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> burned books and works of art in the Bonfire of the Vanities before turning against him; he was hanged and burned in 1498.</p><p>Rule by revelation also brought problems of succession and of minorities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>’s reliance on reincarnation meant long regencies while each new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> grew up, and outside powers manipulated the choice, as when the Qing introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-urn">Golden Urn</a> lottery in 1793. Rule by a single interpretation of religion has borne hardest on women and religious minorities: the Taliban’s Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of 1996–2001 barred girls from school and women from most work, banned music and television, and punished theft by amputation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty.jpg" alt="Lateran Treaty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Group_of_Vatican_and_Italian_government_notables_posing_at_the_Lateran_Palace_before_the_signing_of_the_treaty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The reaction against theocracy shaped the modern state. The principles that government draws its authority from the people and that church and state should be separate ran from the toleration debates of the 17th century to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-amendment-to-the-united-states-constitution">First Amendment to the United States Constitution</a> (1791), the French law on the separation of the churches and the state (1905) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>’s abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in 1924. Yet religious authority kept a constitutional place in many countries. Twenty-six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> sit in the House of Lords, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, which first called itself an Islamic republic in 1956, is generally classed as an Islamic republic rather than a theocracy because secular institutions govern it.</p><p>A few states are usually described as theocracies today. In Vatican City the pope, elected for life by the cardinals under eighty, holds full sovereign power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> is described as a theocratic republic in standard references such as the CIA World Factbook; its Supreme Leader is chosen by the Assembly of Experts, and a constitutional revision of 1989 dropped the requirement that he be a marja, a senior source of religious emulation. The Taliban returned to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in August 2021 and restored the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a>. The monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> still govern their community under Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Tibetan exiles kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> at the head of their administration until 2011, when he transferred his political authority to an elected leader.</p><p>Scholars treat the label with care. Since few states fit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>’s definition, the word is now often used loosely for any government that enforces a religion, sometimes as a polemical charge. Historians debate whether it fits the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, which Runciman called a theocracy, the caliphate or Calvin’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, and many distinguish rule by clergy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a>. The legal scholar Ran Hirschl has described “constitutional theocracies”, states that combine constitutional courts and elections with religion as a source of law, a form he sees spreading since the late 20th century.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Theocracy is government in the name of God, by priests, a sacred ruler or religious law. Named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> for ancient Judea, it took forms as different as the Papal States, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a> and the Islamic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Defenders saw divine law as a guarantee of justice and unity; critics saw unaccountable power and persecution, and modern constitutions mostly ground <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in the people instead.</em></p>

